  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Denis slips into Katya's room to hang the wedding dress back up, only for Katya to catch him. He admits he stole it to stop the wedding, but seeing that she insists she is happy, he steps aside and wishes her and Nikita well.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я про тебя рассказала Кате.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё, я тебя очень жду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Денис, это опять здесь ты?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Я тут ночью к тебе заходил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Я вчера твоё платье украл.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Хотел свадьбу сорвать, но вернул.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что, передумал срывать мою свадьбу?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Скажи честно, ты с ним счастлива?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Денис зашёл пожелать нам счастья.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Да, желаю большого счастья вам.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Спасибо тебе за добрые слова.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Прощай, Денис, и будь счастлив.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я про тебя рассказала Кате.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I already told Katya about you.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'I' and finishing on 'to tell'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё, я тебя очень жду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is all, I am waiting for you eagerly.' Notice how Russian expresses 'everything' and 'am waiting for' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Денис, это опять здесь ты?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Denis, is that you in here again?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'Denis' and finishing on 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Я тут ночью к тебе заходил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I stopped by here at night.' In Russian, the idea of 'I' flows naturally into 'went in' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Я вчера твоё платье украл.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I stole your dress yesterday.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'I' and finishing on 'stole'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Хотел свадьбу сорвать, но вернул.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I wanted to ruin the wedding, but returned it.' In Russian, the idea of 'wanted' flows naturally into 'to give back' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что, передумал срывать мою свадьбу?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What, did you change your mind about ruining my wedding?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'what' and finishing on 'wedding'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Скажи честно, ты с ним счастлива?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Tell me honestly, are you happy with him?' In Russian, the idea of 'say' flows naturally into 'happy' without extra filler words. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Денис зашёл пожелать нам счастья.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Denis stopped by to wish us happiness.' Katya anchors the line on 'Denis', while 'happiness' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Да, желаю большого счастья вам.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I wish great happiness to you both.' The grammar packs 'yes' and 'you' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Спасибо тебе за добрые слова.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you for the kind words.' Notice how Russian expresses 'thank you' and 'words' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Прощай, Денис, и будь счастлив.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Farewell, Denis, and be happy.' Notice how Russian expresses 'farewell' and 'happy' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
